[{:seon.task/name "a-form-has-a-time-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.turn/act!` evaluates through `seon.repl/eval-string` with no per-form bound, so a model's infinite loop holds its run forever. Correct: a config key bounds a form, passed in act!; the interrupt is the named failure point `seon.intr/run` (docs/failure-points.md), and the interrupted form is recorded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-endless-form-is-interrupted-and-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-records-what-it-saw-and-spent"
  :seon.task/plan "A turn records its `:seon.turn/basis` and `:seon.turn/split` (`seon.turn/basis!`), but not the model's raw reply, its finish_reason, model and reasoning, nor the whole split as the agent saw it (4-15 s gaps in a real run had to be traced by hand). Correct: per turn, the messages sent are derivable from the basis; the reply's finish_reason, model and reasoning, and the split context render / model round trip / evals / record / tests are recorded. The dashboard's agent page shows the recorded context, not a second producer of it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turn-records-its-context-reply-and-time-split}} {:seon.task/name "an-outside-context-is-the-turns-context"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.turn/context-block` (the server's read of a task for an outside worktree session) now takes the run's plan and `seon.turn/observe`'s gaps since the branch's merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`), as the turn loop does, so main's pre-existing contract gaps are no longer charged to the agent. It still passes turn n of max-turns 1 to `seon.turn/basis!`, so the context says \"turn 1 of 1\" while the run's env says 8. Correct: context-block passes the run's env max-turns, so the outside session reads exactly what the turn loop's model would. Replaces the second set of basis arguments; nothing new."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-outside-context-names-only-the-branchs-own-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree dry-run's turn profile (2026-10-23, main 8a901ac3, temporary marks, reverted) and the diagnostics timing audit (notes/timing-audit.md, main checkout): every cost that made a scripted e2e-loop turn 17.7 s against a ~100 ms target grew without any record or check seeing it. Measured spans: test-plan's reach walk 4.3 s (5 plans a turn); merge-main's three-way 4.4 s; the writer's branch-diff 2.6 s (blocking the writer); the octopus merge ~1.6 s; problems/caused 0.37 s; a fresh root's first fork queued 3.8-4.8 s behind the fork server's replay of the boot import (d/with 3,504 ms for 42,942 datoms, then load-changes! 420 ms), visible only as that fork's queue time. What exists: an eval records `:ms` and `:alloc-bytes` (`seon.repl/wrap-cost`), no ticks or CPU; a turn stores `:seon.turn/split` {:context-ms :model-ms :eval-ms :record-ms :tests-ms}, rendered nowhere; spawn, merge, branch-diff, a Flow step and the fork server's replay record no measure as a fact. Correct (AGENTS §5, docs/cost.md: work is ticks, bytes and calls, exact; wall and CPU are measured, recorded, never red), re-scoped by the owner's ruling of 2026-10-23 in docs/decisions.md (the model sees each eval's cost, stable across turns so the prompt cache holds: recorded once, shown from that fact; no reused/computed marker): (1) DONE, every eval item's record carries its ticks (`jolt.host/ticks-of`), with :ms, :alloc-bytes and :fns, with :ms, :alloc-bytes and :fns, the values the recording stores (`seon.repl/wrap-cost`, `record-tx`) and the reply shows (`seon.repl/eval-string`), never measured again. Items (2) the turn's split in the context against an estimate, and (3) the fork server's replay, a spawn, merge-main, branch-diff and the octopus merge recording datoms, ticks and ms as a fact, are not taken here: (2) the context's rendering of a cost belongs to the agent-view render, (3) stays a facts-for-the-owner task to file on its own if wanted. Proofs: an eval's record carries :ticks, and the reply item and the stored record carry the same cost values (the render's proof of a stable rendering is the agent-view render's)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-evals-record-carries-its-ticks}}
 {:seon.task/name "context-render-observes-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN step 3 (the agent's turn and its context). `seon.turn/observe` called `seon.reconcile/index` on the branch's db every turn: the whole index listed from empty and settled over every task, though `seon.reconcile/done-input` reads only the agent's task's proofs and kind and the claimants of the tests that ran (notes/perf-2026-10-24.md, Next wins 1: 74% of a 115M-tick, 512 MB context render). Its cost also grew as n^1.75 with the number of tasks. Fix at the cause, with the one incremental mechanism that exists, `seon.reconcile/observe`: `seon.reconcile/index` takes the index of an earlier db and observes the later one over the datoms of the watched attributes that differ (`datahike.api/datoms-diff` per attribute range, which skips the stored subtrees both share), and `seon.turn/index!` holds the agent's last index and db in `seon.agent/state` (:index), so only the first turn lists the index. Deletes the per-turn listing; no new mechanism. Measured on a root's main of 680 tasks and 118k datoms: listing 92.0M ticks and 384 MB, observing 1.29M ticks and 10.2 MB, with equal indexes. The cost proof runs on the in-memory test store, where no node has an address and each diff walks its attribute's datoms, so it holds the linear bound and never the stored db's skip of shared subtrees (measured above, once, by hand). Not taken here: the first turn still lists the index (the fork server could hand main's index to the agents it forks, as it hands :graph)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-index-observed-from-the-last-is-the-index-listed seon.reconcile-test/an-index-is-observed-at-the-cost-of-what-changed}}]
